---
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>4.7 — Informal Run-Time Analysis &amp; The RuntimeAnalyzer Build</h1>

<p>
    <strong>CED Topic 2.12 · 90-Minute Unified Build.</strong> On the AP Computer Science A exam,
    informal run-time analysis asks you to compare algorithms not with a stopwatch, but by counting
    <strong>how many times a key statement executes</strong> as the problem size <code>n</code>
    grows. Today you will build <code>RuntimeAnalyzer.java</code> to instrument linear, logarithmic,
    and quadratic loop structures and prove how their step counts scale.
</p>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Starting Files (Download Both):</strong>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/RuntimeAnalyzer.java" download>RuntimeAnalyzer.java</a> — bare-minimum
            compiling stub.
        </li>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/RuntimeAnalyzerCheck.java" download>RuntimeAnalyzerCheck.java</a> —
            automated AP-style test suite (12 checks across 3 parts).
        </li>
    </ul>
    <pre><code>javac RuntimeAnalyzer.java RuntimeAnalyzerCheck.java
java RuntimeAnalyzerCheck</code></pre>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Parts 1 &amp; 2 · Linear vs. Halving Search</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~45 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 1: <code>countLinearSearchSteps(int n, int targetIndex)</code></h3>
    <p>
        Simulate a linear search over indices <code>0</code> through <code>n - 1</code> looking for
        <code>targetIndex</code>:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>Loop <code>i</code> from <code>0</code> to <code>n - 1</code>.</li>
        <li>On every iteration, increment a <code>steps</code> counter by <code>1</code>.</li>
        <li>If <code>i == targetIndex</code>, immediately return <code>steps</code> (found!).</li>
        <li>If the loop finishes without finding <code>targetIndex</code>, return
            <code>steps</code> (which will equal <code>n</code>).</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Part 2: <code>countBinarySearchSteps(int n, int targetIndex)</code></h3>
    <p>
        Now simulate binary search (halving the search range every step) over the sorted index range
        <code>lo = 0</code> to <code>hi = n - 1</code>:
    </p>
    <pre><code>int lo = 0;
int hi = n - 1;
int steps = 0;
while (lo &lt;= hi) {
    steps++;
    int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;
    if (mid == targetIndex) {
        return steps;
    } else if (mid &lt; targetIndex) {
        lo = mid + 1;
    } else {
        hi = mid - 1;
    }
}
return steps;</code></pre>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint — 10 Steps vs. 1,023 Steps</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Look at Part 2 of <code>RuntimeAnalyzerCheck</code>: when <code>n = 1023</code> and
                <code>targetIndex = 1022</code> (the very last element!), how many steps does
                <code>countLinearSearchSteps(1023, 1022)</code> take vs.
                <code>countBinarySearchSteps(1023, 1022)</code>? Why does doubling <code>n</code>
                from <code>1023</code> to <code>2047</code> add <code>1024</code> steps to linear
                search, but only <strong><code>1</code></strong> step to binary search?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 3 · Discovery &amp; Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~45 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 3: <code>countFullGridSteps(int n)</code> &amp; <code>countUniquePairSteps(int n)</code></h3>

    <p>
        Implement both nested-loop step counters (return <code>0</code> if <code>n &lt;= 0</code>):
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>countFullGridSteps(int n)</code> — counts how many times the inner body runs in a
            full <code>n × n</code> nested loop:
            <code>for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) for (int j = 0; j &lt; n; j++)</code>.</li>
        <li><code>countUniquePairSteps(int n)</code> — counts how many times the inner body runs
            when comparing only unique pairs:
            <code>for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) for (int j = i + 1; j &lt; n; j++)</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Discovery Trap: Does Starting at <code>j = i + 1</code> Escape Quadratic Growth?</h3>
        <p>
            Many students assume that because <code>j = i + 1</code> skips roughly half the grid,
            it is no longer quadratic. Let's check the numbers from your own methods!
        </p>
        <ol>
            <li>Compare <code>countUniquePairSteps(10)</code> (<code>45</code>),
                <code>countUniquePairSteps(100)</code> (<code>4,950</code>), and
                <code>countUniquePairSteps(1000)</code> (<code>499,500</code>). When <code>n</code>
                grows by a factor of <strong>10×</strong> (from 100 to 1,000), by roughly what
                factor does the number of steps grow (10× or 100×)? Why does cutting a quadratic
                algorithm in half still leave it quadratic at scale?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Done When</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><code>java RuntimeAnalyzerCheck</code> reports <strong><code>11 passed, 0 failed</code></strong></li>
            <li>You have completed the reflection boxes above and downloaded your <code>.json</code> for Canvas</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/run-time-analysis.html">Run-Time Analysis</a> — linear, quadratic, and logarithmic step counting</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/loop-analysis.html">Tracing Loops</a></li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
